// The script native player-instant-draw-weapon-by-name (impl exe+0xca0ea0) is bde7c0(ctrl, sid, time, force = 0, 1, arg3) -> PlayerItemController::DrawWeapon
// (exe+0xbde030), whose first gate is `if (CannotSwitchWeapon(ctrl) && !force) return` (exe+0xbe0190: swim / jump / ground-landing tags, player+0x9dc bit 0,
// player+0x50ec bit 1, the controllers at player+0x3218 / +0x30c0 / +0x31f8 / +0x31d0 reporting busy). The native always passes force = 0, so while the
// body is still "busy" from the fist / neck-break / shiv move the draw returns at once with nothing changed (every refusal dump is identical: no pending
// state request, controller idle) and the hands stay empty until the busy state ends by itself (median 2.3 s), when the engine's own pick fills them -
// with whatever the item controller prefers (9 of 64 times another weapon). A gun execution never leaves that state (0 of ~284 refused). The move is over
// at this point (the paired clip ended), so the end restore passes the engine's own force flag: same call, force = 1. Opt-out t2r-no-xr-force-draw.txt.
